<h1>Checkpoints</h1>
<p>A <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#checkpoints">checkpoint</a> is the framework's assertion.
	It takes a condition and a message for each outcome, logs the outcome either way, and moves the case to
	<strong class="status-fail">FAIL</strong> when the condition does not hold. The
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/orcraft-statuses/">Status tests</a> set a status from a single
	observation; the <strong>Checkpoints</strong> test of the same <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group checks
	several distinct things about every run of the Maze application and records each check on its own.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Checkpoints</strong> test and set its <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>. Its matrix is
	the one the Status tests use: a valid and a misspelt command, two heights, and start coordinates that are either
	valid or out of bounds, 16 cases in all. Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="defining-checkpoints">Defining checkpoints</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_statuses_and_results/test_checkpoints/") }}
<p>The test body hands the case to the suite's Maze helper, which runs the application and then checks its
	output:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	maze_app = MazeApp(
		self.config.maze_executable_folder,
		case.params["command"],
		width=case.params["width"],
		height=case.params["height"],
		start_row=case.params["start_row"],
		start_col=case.params["start_col"],
	)

	maze_app.test(case)

	return case.result') }}
<p>The helper's <code>test</code> method runs the application once and passes its output through three checks:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def test(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> None:
	result = self._execute()
	self._test_version_logging(result.stdout, case)
	self._test_path_found_message(result, case)
	self._test_error_messages(result.stderr, case)') }}
<p>Each check ends in a call to <code>case.checkpoint</code>. The first confirms that the application printed its
	version banner and that the banner matches the executable's version. The second applies to a
	<code>solve</code> that produced no error: it confirms that a path was reported and that the number of arrows in
	the drawn path agrees with the step count the application claims. The third compares the application's error
	output with the message the helper expects for the case's inputs:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _test_error_messages(self, stderr: str, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> None:
	expected = self._get_expected_error()
	failure = None if expected in stderr else "Error message is not as expected."
	pass_msg = "Error message matches expected." if stderr else "No error messages as expected."
	fail_msg = failure or ""
	details = f"Expected error message:\\n\\n{expected}" if failure is not None else None
	case.checkpoint(failure is None, pass_msg, fail_msg, details)') }}
<p>The arguments are the condition, the message to log when it holds, the message to log when it does not, and an
	optional <code>details</code> text that is logged with a failure. A valid input is expected to produce no error
	output at all, so the passing message differs depending on whether the application wrote anything.</p>

<h2 id="checkpoints-in-the-log">Checkpoints in the log</h2>
<p>Click <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm. When the run finishes, type
	<code>No error messages as expected</code> into the log's search field and click <strong>Next</strong> to jump
	to the first case:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/checkpoints/passing-checkpoints-in-log.png", "Log of the first Checkpoints case showing the solved maze, the Path found line and four green Checkpoint Pass entries for the version banner, the path-found message, the arrow count and the absence of error messages", "large") }}
<p>Below the solved maze and its <code>Path found</code> line, four <strong>Checkpoint Pass</strong> entries record
	what was checked: the version banner matched, the path-found message was present, the arrow count matched the
	step count, and no error message appeared. A passing case is therefore readable as evidence of what was
	verified, not only as a green status.</p>
<p>Now search for <code>(valid: 0 to 4)</code>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/checkpoints/failed-checkpoint-in-log.png", "Log of a Checkpoints case with an out-of-bounds start column, showing the application's error line, a green Checkpoint Pass for the version banner, a red Checkpoint Fail reading Error message is not as expected, and a Checkpoint Failure Details block with the expected error message", "large") }}
<p>This case asked to solve a maze from column <code>-2</code>. The application refused with
	<em>Start (0,-2) is out of bounds for a 15×5 maze</em>, so the version banner check still passes, and there is
	no path-found check because the run produced an error. The error message check fails: the helper expected
	<em>Start col -2 is out of bounds (valid: 0 to 4)</em>, and the application's wording differs. The log shows the
	red <strong>Checkpoint Fail</strong> entry with the failure message, followed by a
	<strong>Checkpoint Failure Details</strong> block carrying the <code>details</code> text.</p>
<p>A failing checkpoint marks the case <strong class="status-fail">FAIL</strong>, records its message as the case's
	failure description, and then <em>continues</em> with the remaining checks. One case can therefore report every
	problem it found rather than only the first one. When a failed check means the rest of the work would be
	meaningless, <code>is_error=True</code> turns it into an <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> that ends the
	case instead; that option, and how checkpoints behave inside setup and teardown hooks, is covered in the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#checkpoints">framework reference</a>.</p>

<h2 id="the-outcome">The outcome</h2>
<p>Clear the search field. The end of the log holds the test's result and its failures table:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/checkpoints/running-checkpoints-test.png", "Completed Checkpoints run with 16 done, 10 passed and 6 failed, the log showing the test result with the failure description Error message is not as expected and a Failures table listing the six failing cases", "large") }}
<p>The test reports 16 done, 10 passed and 6 failed. The six failures are exactly the <code>solve</code> cases with a
	negative start coordinate: the application rejects the coordinate, but with a different message than the helper
	expects. The <code>slove</code> cases pass, because the application's complaint about the unknown command is the
	one expected, and the two fully valid cases pass every check. The failure description of the test as a whole is
	the message of the first failing checkpoint.</p>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> to see the same outcome per case:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/checkpoints/checkpoints-details.png", "Checkpoints Details view with cases 2, 3, 4, 6, 7 and 8 reading Fail and the other ten reading Pass, and a summary of 16 planned, 10 passed and 6 failed", "large") }}
<p>Cases 2, 3, 4, 6, 7 and 8 read <strong class="status-fail">Fail</strong> with the checkpoint's message in their
	<strong>Failure Description</strong> column; the rest read <strong class="status-pass">Pass</strong>. A
	checkpoint thus does two jobs at once: it decides the status, and it leaves a line in the log that says what was
	checked and what was seen.</p>

<p>Checkpoints report whether a case is right. The next page,
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/result-fields/">Result Fields</a>, shows how a case reports the values it
	measured alongside its status.</p>
